I'm joined today by Corey Duggan who's  professor of sociology at Stone Hill  College and author of the new book kill  it - save it Corey this concept of kill  it - save it it's a concept that in  politics we've heard sort of for  different reasons and in different ways  from the political left and the  political right but speaking generally  what's that concept mean kill it - save  it  well the way I use it in the book is to  explain the current trend towards trying  to dismantle the public sector and the  public infrastructure and in essence the  public good all under the guise of  reform and so the reform narrative and I  think today today or tomorrow we'll find  out about the Republican health care  plan but the idea of essentially  dismantling public support for public  health and public welfare privatizing it  in essence destroying it under the guise  of trying to save it or reform it yeah  and that seems to be I mean would you  agree that that's more of a right-wing  perspective on kill it to save it well I  think that the right wing has really  crystallized this this paradigm and I  think that the you know Donald Trump has  really brought to bear kind of almost  every one and the worst elements what  kill it - save it represent but it's  probably important to note that I wrote  the book assuming Hillary Clinton was  going to be the next president and the  fact that the Democratic Party has  embraced neoliberalism and neoliberalism  is really the foundation of Killah to  save it then I would suggest that it's  not just a right-wing agenda to  privatize the public sector but the  right wing is probably the most  voracious and and in fact the most  successful of doing it the sort of other  more left-wing version of kill it to  save it and I know that this isn't what  the book is about but you're sort of to  put it out there would be the idea that  that we heard from some we actually have  heard it in the lead-up to a number of  presidential elections and sometimes  it's a response to what you're pointing  to as the NiO live  tendencies of the moderate and  mainstream Democratic Party today this  idea of I'm going to vote for whether  it's Ralph Nader  or Jill Stein or whoever even if it  leads to or helps a Republican become  elected president because I support a  total breakdown of order with the longer  term goal of eventually improving  circumstances when everybody realizes  how bad of an idea it is what's your  thought on that I mean that's my idea of  the left wing kill it to save it right I  have to say I think it's a kind of  narrow conception of it and then I'd say  these three things I think I can  remember them all one is that I don't  know all that many leftist so though  there might be some sectarian Maoists  and other folks who really believe the  total breakdown of societies what's  necessary to rebuild it maybe some  anarcho-syndicalist are still out there  hoping for the ashes of the old but that  kind of Neolithic tendency I don't think  is what is behind most of the left wing  of the Democratic Party and the left who  won't vote for the Democratic Party  I would suggest there's also a  principled reason to support other  candidates and that the practicality or  pragmatism that's behind I think the  image that you put forth about that  level of kill to save it on the Left  doesn't hold water in for the same  reasons that I was suggesting that the  Democratic embrace of neoliberalism has  actually brought us Donald Trump I don't  I don't think that the inability of the  Democrats to come up with any actual Pro  public welfare and critical of  capitalist agenda suggests that you know  voting for the Clintons and the Obamas  and the even worse to the right-wing  Democrats has actually gotten us  anywhere but further to the right so I  think there's a there's a there's a  value ethical question about why people  do vote for candidates but I think  there's even a strategical practical  error in that conception of voting for  Democrats who may not be as bad as seem  as bad as the Republicans but  who we should be supporting if we're  really progressive so to dig into more  of the specifics that we're talking  about here why do so many people but  cheer or support candidates or policies  that are going to actually not be great  for them personally but also more  broadly sort of hurt democracy as we  know it as a sort of the the most  representative possible form of  democracy we saw this in 2010 with the  Tea Party by and large supporting  candidates whose policies would benefit  them if they one day became rich which  of course statistically most of them  never ever will there's many many  examples of this I mean why do people do  that  well in the book I suggest there's a  kind of ideology that's been hardwired  over the last 50 years but particularly  during the Reagan administration and  really the rise of neoliberalism that's  combined what I call hyper individualism  this notion of we are best when we are  most free and we're most free when we  are unrestrained by government programs  unburdened by public responsibilities  give us all of our money back we don't  want to pay taxes because the government  doesn't know what to do that I know what  to do with it and then linking eiper  individualism with a kind of corporate a  dominant way of thinking that suggests  you know whatever's good for  corporations is clearly good for us and  so while there are lots of  contradictions in those two things fused  together it means that people  consistently vote against their best  interests because they don't have any  faith or belief in the public sector  anymore but of course as individuals we  can't build roads and we can't fix  bridges and we can't improve our  educational system and we could go on  and on down the line but the best this  country and really the world has ever  had to offer people was when we  recognized our mutual interests or  mutual self-interest and the need for us  as people to come together to build the  large things that societies need and  since you  Ronald Reagan I mean there's definitely  some aspect of the importance of myths  that that enter people's minds when they  conceptualized either the role of  government or welfare programs or  whatever poverty for example I mean with  Reagan there was this welfare queen who  drove a Cadillac I mean I don't even  remember the specifics of it at this  point  more broadly there's this idea that oh  poor people are the only source  responsible for their own poverty  there's nothing systemic at all that we  can point to or class mobility or  whatever the case may be and these myths  have been pretty successfully been  perpetrated and repeated over the last  many decades haven't they  that's right and they are a sort of the  big lie in the sense that the more often  they're repeated the more likely they  are that people will believe them and I  do think it's really hard especially  this particular historical juncture for  a lot of people in the United States  Americans and particularly white  Americans to kind of think about the  historical and structural ways in which  working people people of color have been  oppressed and discriminated again and I  actually just wrote a piece in which  looking at a case here in Massachusetts  where the Massachusetts Supreme Court  actually found that it was legal and one  should not in fact prejudice in court  against African American men who flee  from the police during questioning or  were being asked to stop because because  racial profiling has reached such a  degree in places like Boston and  Baltimore in Ferguson and actually you  could go through every state and every  city in the country that actually had  studied this issue the Department of  Justice has done a number of these  studies and find that racial profiling  is alive and well and so brilliant and  so detrimental particularly to  african-american men that it is in fact  justifiable for them to flee this is  what the Supreme Court of Massachusetts  found and so the idea that we have still  hardwired streams of races  and the streams of class exploitation  should come as no surprise big surprise  is that we are so many people are so  happy to have the entitlement of race  the entitlement of being white that  they'll also try to ignore that it  actually still exists and the same is  true with class exploitation  you know Ronald Reagan successfully  started the trend towards dismantling  unions and delegitimizing unions when  collective bargaining and collective  power among workers was really what the  middle class was built off we've been  speaking about the book kill it to save  it whose author has been talking to us  Cory  Dogen professor of sociology at Stone  Hill College I highly recommend the book  so great talking to you about this today  thank you so much David
 

